  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nWV imagery showing short-wave now extending north-south through \ncentral New Mexico with layered moisture to the east over the \neastern half of the CWA. PWs are as high as 1.2 inches in this \nlayered moisture. Further west, the main upper trough extends from \nthe Great Basin low down across central Arizona. So the far east \nstill has a risk of thunderstorms today/tonight. Sacramento Mtns \ncould see a stray storm this afternoon, with Hudspeth seeing some \nisolated this evening. HRRR showing good outflow hitting west \nTexas early this evening to aid in that development. \n\nSunday and Monday...look to remain dry as that main upper trough and \nmoisture plume move well east of our area. Highest PWs remain in the \neast, but only around .70 inches. Probably non-zero chances for a \nstorm over Otero/Hudspeth Counties but will not mention this in \nthe grids.\n\nTuesday through Friday...abundant sub-tropical moisture rapidly \nstreams up from the south with the help of a sub-tropical jet (80+ \nkts by Thursday). PWs approaching 1.50 inches Tuesday night (record \nlevels) and only back off slightly Wednesday through Friday. Thus \nexpect widespread showers/thunderstorms all these days. Small scale \nfeatures will determine exactly when the heaviest rain and flood \npotential occur. GFS shows tropical wave/vort center moving out of \nMexico to our area Tuesday night, though the ECMWF not as visible \nwith this. ECMWF has been consistent in showing TS remnants \n(Odalys?) moving up Thursday night. Both these features could really \nenhance an already primed situation. Flood Watches will likely be \nissued eventually for this period. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...GFS/ECMWF patterns diverge noticeably, but \ntheir bottom lines are about the same; less moisture but still \nexpect isolated/scattered thunderstorms each day, though Sunday \ntransitioning downward with activity.",
